      "text": "The largest such triangle will have one side that is the largest of the diagonals. Since the diagonals of a rectangle are equal, the largest diagonal is $11^2+10^2+2\\cdot 10\\cdot 11 \\sqrt{3}/2$ which simplifies to $468 \\sqrt{3}$.\nSince we want the maximum side of the triangle though, we take the side opposite that diagonal, which simplifies to $467 \\sqrt{3}$. The area of an equilateral triangle is $S=(1/4)d^2$, which eliminates this field to us needing to find $\\sqrt{467 \\sqrt{3}}$ for our side. $467 \\sqrt{3} \\approx 334.9$, so the answer is just $\\sqrt{334^2} = {334}$ longer Question:\nLet $S_{ABCD}$ and $S_{PQRS}$ be two overlapping square, each of side $12$ centimeters, such that $RS \\parallel AB$ and $QS \\parallel AD$.  What is the greatest possible length $SR$?\n\nWithout loss of generality, draw $PA$ perpendicular to $AC$ and let it intersect $AD$ at $E$. We have that $AE$ is parallel to $BC$, and is therefore of length $12$. Let $M$ be the midpoint of $AB$. We take triangle $AEM$ to be an isosceles triangle with $ME = 6$ and $AE$ as the height. We see that $\\sin(90^{\\circ}-\\alpha) = \\frac{6}{12}=1/2$, and so $\\alpha = 30^{\\circ}$, and that the length of $SR$ is $SR = 12\\cos(30^{\\circ}) = 12(3^{\\frac{1}{2}}/2) = 6\\sqrt{3}$ centimeters.",
