      "text": "Let the equilateral triangle be $PQR$. Since the points of the equilateral triangle $PQR$ can't be outside $ABCD$, we must have all the vertices $P, Q, R, A, B, C, D$ inside the rectangle. Additionally, the $4$ vertices that fall outside the rectangle are $P, Q$ (assuming that $P$ and $Q$ are on the longer side of the rectangle because the shorter side would be less than $10)$, sides $PA, BC, RQ$. To find the maximum area of the triangle we must first generate the largest possible triangle we can draw inside the rectangle. First we can see that to do this there are two factors we need to consider: tangent and length. Since the triangle must be inscribed in the rectangle it must have all vertices guaranteed to be inside the rectangle, this means that we have to reverse the order of the sides' lengths as otherwise the right angle won't fit inside the rectangle since the sides must have lengths of $10$ and $11$\n\nLooking at the triangle, we see that it is an equilateral triangle with $10$ and $11$ sides. If we flip the area this way we can just look at the triangle geometry inside the rectangle to find that $S_{PQR}=\\sqrt{ *S_{AFC}}$, $S_{AFC}=\\frac{1}{2}bh=\\frac{1}{2}10x$, $x=\\frac{10}{11}$, $S_{AFC}=\\frac{1}{2}\\times (0.1)x$.  I will start on finding tangent. $RQ$ and side $BC$ have length $11$ lets call this $h$. So $RQ>BC$, so the height must be larger than $10$, giving a maximum height of $\\frac{10}{11}$. The largest of the hypotenuses would be $BC$ so that $x=\\frac{10}{11}$. $a^2+b^2=c^2$ and with the side lengths we know we get $a=\\sqrt{h^2-b^2}=\\sqrt{h^2-\\left(\\frac{10}{11}\\right)^2}$, but since we have to flip the area so as not to put the smaller side aspect of the rectangle as our bottom (negative area) we have to reverse all the ages so $a=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{10}{11}\\right)^2}-h=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{10}{11}\\right)^2}-\\frac{10}{11}$.\n\nRunning this into the tangent formula, $T_{PQR}=\\frac{2S_{PQR}}{T_{ABC}-T_{RQD}}=\\frac{2\\sqrt*S_{AFC}}{\\frac{10}{11}+\\frac{10}{11}}=\\frac{(\\sqrt{30}-\\sqrt{22})}{10}$ Now running into final equation, $S_{PQR}=\\sqrt{*S_{AFC}}=\\sqrt90\\sqrt{\\frac{30}{22}-1}=\\sqrt{\\frac{(30)^2(30-22)}{22}}=\\boxed{20(30-22)}$",
